# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import VX

struct Loop: ~Copyable {
  init() throws(LoopError)
  mutating func wait(until: Deadline? = nil, leeway: Duration = .zero) -> Events
  mutating func poll() -> Events
  var remote: LoopRemote { get }        // wake() and post(_:) from any thread
  var wakeups: UInt64 { get }

  mutating func timer(at: Deadline, leeway: Duration = .zero,
                      repeating: Duration? = nil) -> TimerID
  mutating func cancel(_ id: TimerID)

  mutating func openWindow(_ title: String, width: Int32, height: Int32)
    throws(WindowError) -> WindowID
  mutating func openPopup(_ owner: WindowID, _ title: String,
                          x: Int32, y: Int32, width: Int32, height: Int32)
    throws(WindowError) -> WindowID
  mutating func requestFrame(_ id: WindowID)
  mutating func cpuSurface(_ id: WindowID) -> CPUSurface?
  mutating func present(_ id: WindowID, _ surface: consuming CPUSurface,
                        configSeq: UInt64, damage: (x: Int32, y: Int32,
                        width: Int32, height: Int32)? = nil) throws(WindowError)
  mutating func closeWindow(_ id: WindowID)
  // setTitle, setFullscreen, setPanel, setApp, placeWindow, setDesktop,
  // setPopupArea

  mutating func listen(port name: String) throws(RouteError) -> ListenID
  func route(_ message: RouteMessage) throws(RouteError)
}

func processNamespace() -> Namespace?
func processArguments() -> [String]
```

# DESCRIPTION

VX is the SDK's core: what a program uses to wait, to show windows and to take their input, and to plumb.
It is tier 0, so a service links it as an app does.

A `Loop` is one port, and `wait` returns what happened since the last as `Events`, each with its window (`WindowID.none` for the loop's own), the time it was taken and its place in the loop's sequence.
A timer fires at its deadline, never before, and may be up to its leeway late so that nearby timers share a wakeup; a repeating one is re-armed from its deadline, not from when it fired, so it does not drift, and `missed` says how many repeats a late loop skipped.
`wait(until:)` returns at its deadline if nothing comes sooner.
Another thread reaches the loop through its `remote`: `post` delivers a `Message`, two words, as a `.message` event, in order, and `wake` a `.wake`, wakes before the loop runs making one.
Timers and frames are the port wait's deadline, so a loop with nothing due waits with none and wakes no times; `wakeups` counts its waits that blocked, each a `loop.wait` zone in the trace.

`openWindow` opens a window of Radharc's (radharc(4)) at `/svc/windows/compositor`, which a manifest reaches with `use windows`; its first `.configure` comes as an event, with its size in points and in pixels, its scale (120 is 1×), whether it has focus, and the `configSeq` to present for.
`requestFrame` asks for one `.frame`: Radharc's answer to the present in flight, or, with none in flight, the loop's own at once (`estimated`), aimed at the next vblank it can still make.
A frame's `target` is when the next present should reach the screen, `presentedAt` when the last did, and `refresh` the period.
`cpuSurface` gives one of the window's two buffers at its configured size (nil while both are being read), 0x00RRGGBB in sRGB, with `age`, the presents since it was last shown, for a partial redraw; `present` shows it at the next vblank, and refuses one drawn for another configure (`staleConfig`), which is never stretched.
Keys come as `.keyDown` and `.keyUp` with the key's HID usage (`KeyUsage`), its unshifted character in the layout and the modifiers after it, the pointer as `.pointer` in the window's points with its buttons, and the wheel as `.wheel`, a notch 10 points; only the focused window has keys.
The close box is `.close`, and the program decides; so is a popup's owner closing.
A window that placed itself hears `.moved`.

If Radharc ends, the program carries on: its presents are kept, its frames come from the loop's estimate, and every 20 ms the loop tries the compositor again.
Once the launcher has restarted it, each window is opened again with its title, its place and its role, its images added and its last present shown, and the program hears one `.configure`; a popup is closed instead.

`route` sends a `RouteMessage` (the fields of plumber(4)'s messages) to the plumber at `/mnt/plumb`, whose rules choose its port; `refused` if none takes it.
`listen` opens one of the plumber's ports, and what is sent there comes as `.routed` events, from then until the program ends.
Each port is read on a thread of its own, which hands its messages to the loop.

`minimal` is the SDK's reference app: a window whose colour changes with each frame, quitting on escape or its close box, in nine calls (`Loop`, `openWindow`, `requestFrame`, `wait`, `cpuSurface`, `fill`, `present`, `requestFrame` and `closeWindow`).
Given a number, it quits after that many frames.
The boot test boot-sdk runs it beside bin/sdk-test, which checks the loop's timers, posts and wakes, a window's configure, frames, surface, key and pointer, Radharc killed and the window back, routing through the plumber, and that an idle loop wakes only at its deadline.

